    feat(growth): auth, tenancy, and Supabase persistence architecture
    
    Highest-priority gap from Phase 1 closed: /command was an unauthenticated
    operational console.
    
    AUTH
    - src/middleware.ts gates /command and /api/growth/**. It activates
      updateSession(), which existed since before this work and nothing ever called:
      there was no middleware, so the session helper was dead code and every route
      decided for itself. The matcher is deliberately narrow, because a site-wide one
      would put a Supabase round trip in front of public marketing pages.
    - lib/growth/auth/session.ts resolves a real Supabase identity into the Phase 1
      Principal, reading memberships from knoux_growth_memberships.
    - API routes re-check server-side. Middleware sees presence-of-session, which is
      not the same question as workspace authorisation, and a route must not depend on
      a matcher continuing to cover it.
    - /api/intelligence now checks the client boundary against the client named in
      the request body. intelligence.use alone must not authorise reasoning about any
      workspace.
    
    The invariant under test: an authenticated user with no membership row gets
    nothing. No default grant, no fallthrough to all-clients. A provisioning gap reads
    as a provisioning gap, as its own NOT_PROVISIONED state, and a membership table
    that is not deployed reads as MEMBERSHIP_UNAVAILABLE rather than as access denied.
    
    PERSISTENCE
    - Two additive migrations following the repository's conventions exactly
      (knoux_ prefix, if not exists, named constraints, explicit anon revoke).
      RLS on every table, all policies resolving through membership.
    - Three constraints encode safety rules the application already enforced, so the
      schema refuses them too: self-approval, LIVE without provider evidence, and
      CONNECTED without a verification time. The audit log has insert-read only, no
      update or delete policy.
    - growth_communities has no column for member data, so no query can select it.
    - persistence/repository.ts defines the interface with two implementations.
      There is no silent fallback: a configured production store that fails returns
      an explicit failure, because a database blip silently becoming demo data is
      exactly how fixture numbers become client numbers.
    - Migrations are written and NOT applied. No environment was proven safe to apply
      them to. See docs/growth/PERSISTENCE.md.

